From: Devin <devin@cognition.ai>
Date: Thu, 23 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] stamp-p4: vfork child must only exec or _exit(127)

Issue #54.  Under DROPBEAR_VFORK the child shares the parent's entire
address space.  On the way to execv() the child ran clearenv(), ~10x
addnewvar()/putenv(), m_strdup()/m_free() and other libc calls - all
writing the shared heap, and the shared __environ/last_environ globals.
Any of those writes - or a failed execv() that then returned -1 back
through dropbear frames into dropbear_exit()'s vsnprintf/syslog/stdio -
can scribble on shared state including uClibc's sighandler[] table.
The parent's next SIGCHLD then dispatches through a corrupted entry
(fault observed at the jalr inside pthread_sighandler) -> wild jump ->
SIGILL, killing the session process.

Fix: the vfork child does NO libc work at all - only plain syscalls.
The session environment is built by the parent *before* vfork into a
private envp array (build_child_envp), and the child execve()s with it
directly.  All child-side error paths end in _exit(127) with no logging,
no stdio, no unwinding into parent frames.

SIGPIPE is still reset for the exec'd child - the server sets it to
SIG_IGN in commonsetup() and SIG_IGN survives execve - but via a raw
rt_sigaction syscall: vfork has no CLONE_SIGHAND, so only the child's
kernel sigaction changes and the shared sighandler[] table is untouched.
